ring\"}}, \"required\": []}}}\n{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"browser_wait\", \"description\": \"Wait up to ms milliseconds (default 1000, max 15000), or until the element matched by selector appears, then return the page state. Useful after starting a server or triggering a slow update.\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"ms\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}, \"selector\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": []}}}\n{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"browser_new_page\", \"description\": \"Open an ADDITIONAL page in the same browser session and make it the active page, so a later browser_switch_page can come back to the earlier one. Pages behave like tabs of one browser: they share cookies and local storage, but each holds its own DOM, its own JavaScript state and its own open network connections. Optionally navigate the new page to url immediately. All other browser_* tools always act on the active page.\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"url\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": []}}}\n{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"browser_switch_page\", \"description\": \"Make an already-open page the active one, so subsequent browser_* calls act on it. page is a label such as 'page-1' (the labels are listed in the OPEN PAGES line of any browser result once more than one page is open). Returns that page's current structure.\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"page\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"page\"]}}}\n{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"browser_close_page\", \"description\": \"Close an open page, given its label (e.g. 'page-2'); with no argument, closes the active page. Another open page becomes active if there is one.\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"page\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": []}}}\n</tools>\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<tool_call>\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>\nvalue_1\n</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format: an inner <function=...></function> block must be nested within <tool_call></tool_call> XML tags\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n</IMPORTANT>\n\nYou are a software engineer. Your workspace directory is /tmp/runs/smokes/qwen-q4/message-board/interactive/runs/101/app. Implement the task described by the user by creating files in the workspace using the provided tools. Work until the task is complete, then call finish. Use only the tools provided to you.\n\nEnvironment: the tools listed in this request are the complete set available to you. Tools not listed do not exist in this environment and cannot be installed or obtained. Review what is available and use whatever helps you complete and check your work. If execution tools are provided, they give you a live development environment: the commands you run through them execute for real in your workspace, on a machine with Node.js and npm installed and the npm registry reachable, so the packages you install and the servers you start are real. If no installation or execution tools are provided, author every required file completely from first principles; dependencies declared in package.json files are provisioned by the environment after you finish.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\n## Context\n\nWe are building a traditional client-server web application with real-time capabilities. To minimize external dependencies while retaining the power of PostgreSQL, we are using PGLite embedded within a Node.js server. Real-time updates will be pushed to the client using Server-Sent Events (SSE).\n\n## Goals / Non-Goals\n\n**Goals:**\n- Provide a simple message board where users can post text messages.\n- Ensure all connected clients receive updates instantly when new messages are posted.\n- Store data persistently on the local disk using PGLite.\n- Keep the frontend incredibly simple and lightweight.\n\n**Non-Goals:**\n- User authentication and authorization.\n- Rich text formatting or media attachments.\n- Horizontal scaling across multiple server instances (PGLite is tied to the single Node instance).\n\n## Decisions\n\n**Decision 1: Embedded PGLite vs External Postgres**\n- *Rationale*: PGLite allows us to run PostgreSQL directly inside the Node.js process. This means users don't need to install or configure a separate database service. It seamlessly handles raw SQL queries and can persist to the filesystem.\n\n**Decision 2: Server-Sent Events (SSE) vs WebSockets**\n- *Rationale*: We only need one-way real-time communication (Server pushing new messages to Clients). Clients can post new messages via standard HTTP POST requests. SSE is much simpler to implement, works natively over standard HTTP, and doesn't require a complex WebSocket library like Socket.io.\n\n**Decision 3: Vanilla JS / Vite vs React**\n- *Rationale*: Given the minimal UI requirements (a message list and a text input), Vanilla JS keeps the build extremely fast and the dependency tree small. Vite provides a great developer experience out of the box.\n\n## Risks / Trade-offs\n\n- [Risk] PGLite performance on very large datasets: Embedded WASM databases are incredibly fast but might struggle if the dataset grows to tens of gigabytes.\n  - *Mitigation*: This is a local application and we expect message volume to be manageable for a single server instance.\n- [Risk] SSE connection limits: Browsers have limits on the number of concurrent SSE connections per domain.\n  - *Mitigation*: We only need one SSE connection per client tab, which is well within browser limits.\n\n## Why\n\nWe need a fast, local-first collaborative message board that allows users to post messages and see updates in real-time. This provides an incredibly low-latency experience by keeping data local while ensuring all users see the same state instantly. It serves as an excellent demonstration of using embedded PGLite combined with Server-Sent Events (SSE) to create a robust, lightweight realtime application without external database dependencies.\n\n## What Changes\n\n- We are introducing a brand new traditional client-server web application.\n- The backend will be a Node.js server using an embedded PGLite database for raw SQL queries, persisting data to the local disk.\n- Real-time synchronization will be handled via Server-Sent Events (SSE).\n- The frontend will be a simple, lightweight Single Page Application (Vanilla JS or React via Vite).\n\n## Capabilities\n\n### New Capabilities\n- `realtime-board`: The core message board capability covering posting messages, viewing a message feed, and receiving live updates via SSE.\n\n### Modified Capabilities\nNone (Greenfield project).\n\n## Impact\n\nThis is a brand new project. It will establish the foundational architecture including the Node.js backend setup, the embedded PGLite database structure, the SSE connection handling, and the frontend web UI.\n\n## 1. Project Initialization\n\n- [ ] 1.1 Initialize the Node.js project and install backend dependencies (express, @electric-sql/pglite, cors)\n- [ ] 1.2 Initialize the Vanilla JS / Vite project for the frontend and install frontend dependencies\n- [ ] 1.3 Set up basic npm scripts for concurrently running backend and frontend development servers\n- [ ] 1.4 Make the app start from a fresh checkout with no manual steps: once the dependencies declared in its package.json files are installed, `npm run dev` at the project root starts both the backend and the frontend, the backend creates any directory it needs (such as PGLite's data directory), and the page the frontend serves reaches the backend's API and event stream\n\n## 2. Backend Infrastructure & Database\n\n- [ ] 2.1 Set up a basic Express server with CORS and JSON body parsing\n- [ ] 2.2 Initialize embedded PGLite configured to write to the local file system\n- [ ] 2.3 Create the database initialization script (create `messages` table with `id`, `text`, `created_at`)\n- [ ] 2.4 Create the historical messages endpoint (`GET /api/messages`) to fetch initial state\n\n## 3. Real-time Capabilities (SSE)\n\n- [ ] 3.1 Implement the SSE endpoint (`GET /api/stream`) that maintains active connections with clients\n- [ ] 3.2 Implement the messaging endpoint (`POST /api/messages`) to insert text into PGLite\n- [ ] 3.3 Broadcast new messages to all active SSE connections when a new message is successfully inserted\n\n## 4. Frontend Implementation\n\n- [ ] 4.1 Create the basic HTML layout containing a message list and a text input form\n- [ ] 4.2 Fetch the message history (`GET /api/messages`) on initial page load and render messages\n- [ ] 4.3 Connect to the SSE endpoint (`GET /api/stream`) using `EventSource` to listen for new messages\n- [ ] 4.4 Append incoming real-time messages to the DOM seamlessly\n- [ ] 4.5 Wire up the form submission to send a `POST` request to `/api/messages` and clear the input<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\nI'll build this message board application step by step. Let me start by exploring the workspace and setting up the project structure.\n\n<tool_call>\n<function=list_dir>\n<parameter=path>\n.\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call><|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\n<tool_response>\n(empty)\n</tool_response><|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n<tool_call>\n<function=write_file>\n<parameter=path>\npackage.json\n</parameter>\n<parameter=content>\n{\n  \"name\": \"message-board\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"description\": \"Real-time message board using PGLite and SSE\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"dev:backend\": \"node server.js\",\n    \"dev:frontend\": \"vite --host\",\n    \"dev\": \"npm run dev:backend & npm run dev:frontend\"\n  },\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"@electric-sql/pglite\": \"^0.2.4\",\n    \"cors\": \"^2.8.5\",\n    \"express\": \"^4.18.2\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"vite\": \"^5.0.0\"\n  }\n}\n\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call><|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\n<tool_response>\nwrote 424 chars to package.json\n</tool_response><|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n<tool_call>\n<function=run_command>\n<parameter=command>\ncd /tmp/runs/smokes/qwen-q4/message-board/interactive/runs/101/app && npm install 2>&1\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call><|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\n<tool_response>\n$ cd /tmp/runs/smokes/qwen-q4/message-board/interactive/runs/101/app && npm install 2>&1\n[exit 0]\nSTDOUT:\n\nadded 82 packages, and audited 83 packages in 3s\n\n20 packages are looking for funding\n  run `npm fund` for details\n\n3 vulnerabilities (1 moderate, 2 high)\n\nTo address issues that do not require attention, run:\n  npm audit fix\n\nTo address all issues (including breaking changes), run:\n  npm audit fix --force\n\nRun `npm audit` for details.\nnpm warn install-scripts 1 package had install scripts blocked because they are not covered by allowScripts:\nnpm warn install-scripts   esbuild@0.21.5 (postinstall: node install.js)\nnpm warn install-scripts\nnpm warn install-scripts Run `npm install-scripts ls` to review, or `npm install-scripts approve <pkg>` to allow.\n</tool_response><|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n<tool_call>\n<function=run_command>\n<parameter=command>\ncd /tmp/runs/smokes/qwen-q4/message-board/interactive/runs/101/app && npm install-scripts approve esbuild@0.21.5 2>&1\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call><|im_end|>\n<|im_star